  ],
  "text": "There are two high tides every day, and the Moon is only ever on one side. Come back six hours later and the whole sea has left. The Moon pulls hardest on whatever is nearest to it. So the ocean facing the Moon is lifted into a wide mound of water. Which explains one high tide. Not two. And this is the part almost nobody gets told. The Moon pulls on the solid Earth too, and the rock is closer than the far ocean. So the planet gets tugged away, and the far ocean is simply left behind. Two bulges. And the Earth turns through both of them every single day. The sea is not sloshing back and forth. You are being carried into a standing hill of it. The Sun joins in. Line all three up and the tides run higher still. So the tide is not the sea rising. It is you turning underneath it. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
